---
import ContentCardEmpty from "@components/cards/ContentCardEmpty.astro";
import CardboardForeground from "@assets/cardboard/foreground.webp";
import CardboardBackground from "@assets/cardboard/background.webp";

import CardboardLayout from "@layouts/CardboardLayout.astro";
import Center from "@components/Center.astro";
import PaperBackground from "@components/ball_in_a_box/PaperBackground.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import Box from "@assets/gallery/box.webp";
import MenuBackground from "@assets/gallery/menu_background.webp";
import Button from "@assets/gallery/button.webp";
import Slider from "@assets/gallery/slider.webp";
import Side from "@assets/gallery/side.webp";
import Balls from "@assets/gallery/balls.png";
import WebBackground from "@assets/gallery/web_background.webp";
import Paper from "@assets/gallery/paper.webp";
import TutorialButtons from "@assets/gallery/tutorial_buttons.webp";
import Loading from "@assets/gallery/loading.webp";

import BallsInspiration from "@assets/gallery/BallsInspiration.webp";
import EarlyBlue from "@assets/gallery/early_blue.png";
---

<CardboardLayout title="Ball in a Box Gallery">
    <ContentCardEmpty>
        <Center>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Box} alt="" loading="eager" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        This is the box that is used as the games background. I
                        actually folded up that box myself, since I didn't have
                        a good square box laying around at the time.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Usually when I took photos for the game, I simply put
                        them on the floor. But for this one I had to hold it,
                        because I wanted the light source to come from the top
                        left. Putting it on the floor would make it have way
                        softer shadows and not turn out the way I wanted it to.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Because I had to hold it, it became tricky to take a
                        good picture. The box ended up not entirely facing the
                        camera, so I ended up fixing it afterwards in Krita.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Afterwards, I adjusted the colors to make it look nicer,
                        and also adjusted some smaller details. For example, I
                        straightened the slit in the middle, and also removed
                        the hole in the middle, since I thought it looked nicer.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Side} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        This is what's used as a border around the cardboard
                        box. I used all of my 4 Minecraft books to straighten
                        out the cardboard as much as possible when taking a
                        picture. Afterwards, I simply adjusted the colors to
                        fit, and cleaned it up a bit, as there were a lot of
                        small bits of cardboard sticking out.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={MenuBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>This is the background used for the menu.</p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Button} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>This is the button used inside the menu.</p>

                    <p>
                        In the game, the buttons are actually a bit stretched
                        since the text inside wouldn't fit otherwise. Luckily,
                        it's not too noticeable if you don't think about it.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Slider} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        This is the slider background and the slider handle used
                        inside the settings menu.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The slider background is a bit transparent around the
                        edges to try to have it blend in to the menu background.
                        The goal was to have it look like the front part of the
                        cardboard got cut off to expose the internal wavy
                        cardboard.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The slider background also got trimmed quite a bit to
                        try to make it thinner and longer so I wouldn't have to
                        stretch it. I ended up having to stretch it in-game
                        anyways.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The slider handle is a small piece of white colored
                        cardboard. I'm not too proud of the final result, but it
                        works.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Balls} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        These are the different textures for each of the
                        different balls in the game.
                    </p>
                    <details>
                        <summary>
                            <strong>See inspiration for the balls</strong>
                        </summary>
                        I unfortunately couldn't find the exact or very similar pumpkins
                        that inspired the first few designs.
                        <br />
                        <Image
                            src={BallsInspiration}
                            alt="Inspiration for ball design"
                        />
                    </details>
                    <p>
                        The first balls I worked on were the blue and green
                        balls. They were originally inspired by the "goofy ahh
                        pumpkin" memes that were popular at the time. The green
                        ball was also inspired by the green meme ball sometimes
                        seen in some Roblox games.
                    </p>
                    <details>
                        <summary>
                            <strong>See early blue ball design</strong>
                        </summary>
                        The reason this wasn't showcased in the above image was because
                        I couldn't find its image. Only a screenshot of it in-game.
                        <br />
                        <Image
                            src={EarlyBlue}
                            alt="Early design for blue ball"
                        />
                    </details>
                    <p>
                        The problem was that I wasn't really good at drawing
                        those types of faces. After some time, they ended up
                        looking too similar to the original inspiration images I
                        had, so I ended up rethinking the design.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I tried taking inspiration from my squid plush I had
                        nearby. I ended up making a smiling blue ball, and a
                        frowning orange ball. Unfortunately they were too
                        similar to my inspiration again.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I decided to go back to the green ball and try to make
                        something original, still with the inspiration of the
                        green meme ball. I quite liked the results, as it wasn't
                        too similar to my inspiration this time.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        After that, I decided to go back to the blue ball and
                        try to redesign the face, but with the style from the
                        green ball. It was successful once again! Lastly, I
                        decided to redo the orange ball (which had now turned
                        red) with the inspiration of the red meme ball sometimes
                        seen in some Roblox games.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Once that was all done, I decided to go through all the
                        balls again and cleaning up the lines, adding some more
                        detail and adjusting some things. And that's how the
                        final balls were made!
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={TutorialButtons} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        Since the game was borderless, there wasn't an obvious
                        way to move the window around or to close the game. To
                        fix this, I made a simple tutorial which would show you
                        that you could drag the window by simply clicking on it,
                        and then that you could press the escape key to open a
                        menu. This would only happen once, and only if the user
                        hadn't already moved the box or opened the menu after a
                        few seconds.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The web version of Ball in a Box doesn't actually have
                        the second part of the tutorial. That is because I found
                        it annoying having to open the menu to make it stop.
                        There were also obvious ways to close the box this time,
                        such as clicking the "Close box" button, or just
                        refreshing the page.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="dark-container">
                <Image src={WebBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        This is the background of the games itch.io page, as
                        well as this page!
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Some extra work had to be done to make it tile nicely
                        without being too noticeable. I didn't do a very good
                        job at the time. It's quite easy to see it tile since
                        the fade from one side to the other lacks quite a bit of
                        detail.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The reason why there was a lack of detail was because
                        the lighting was a bit inconsistent. I ended up making
                        the fade be very large, as well as painting it with a
                        soft low opacity brush to make it more consistent.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        On the itch.io page, I didn't really do much to try to
                        hide it. But on this website, I scaled and moved it so
                        that the seam would be in the middle, which is where the
                        content is! So you don't really notice it that much.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Paper} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        This background was recently made as I was making my
                        website. It's used to make text a bit more readable, as
                        the text was simply just above the cardboard before.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This time, I did a way better job with making the tiling
                        less noticeable. I managed to make the lightness more
                        consistent across the whole image, so the fade between
                        the edges is only the size of 1 square.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <Image src={Loading} alt="" loading="lazy" />
                <PaperBackground>
                    <p>
                        When porting the game to the web, I noticed assets were
                        loading in way slower than on desktop. To fix this, I
                        created a simple loading screen which would display
                        while the game was loading.
                    </p>
                </PaperBackground>
            </div>
        </Center>
    </ContentCardEmpty>
</CardboardLayout>

<style
    define:vars={{
        cardboard: `url("${CardboardForeground.src}")`,
        darkcardboard: `url("${CardboardBackground.src}")`,
    }}
>
    img {
        width: max-content;
        background-color: black;
        max-width: 100%;
        border-radius: 12px;
        object-fit: scale-down;
        height: min-content;
        box-shadow: 0 0 12px -4px;

        @media (max-width: 600px) {
        }
    }

    .dark-container {
        background-image: var(--darkcardboard);
        background-repeat: repeat;
        background-size: 125%;
        padding: 12px;
        border-radius: 24px;
        background-color: white;
        box-shadow: 0 0 18px -6px;
        margin-bottom: 24px;

        @media (max-width: 600px) {
            padding-left: 0px;
            padding-right: 0px;
            padding-top: 0px;
        }
    }

    .container {
        background-image: var(--cardboard);
        background-repeat: repeat;
        background-size: 125%;
        padding: 12px;
        border-radius: 24px;
        background-color: white;
        box-shadow: 0 0 18px -6px;
        margin-bottom: 24px;

        @media (max-width: 600px) {
            padding-left: 0px;
            padding-right: 0px;
            padding-top: 0px;
        }
    }
</style>
